course: dictionary
lesson: 15994
title: Words for saying things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Writers often choose a special word for speaking instead of the plain word said. Uttered means said something out loud, or made a sound with the voice. «She uttered a cry.» Here uttered tells you she made a sound with her voice.
explain: Uttered often shows that only a little was said. «The old man uttered a single word and then closed his eyes.» Only one word came out, so the writer uses uttered to show it was quiet.
explain: Look at the words around uttered to check its meaning. «He never uttered a word» means he stayed silent and said nothing at all.
fact: Uttered is the past tense of utter, which means to say something out loud.
fact: «She uttered a cry.» means she made a sound with her voice.
fact: «Never uttered a word» means stayed silent.
fact: Writers use uttered mostly in stories, when they want to show how someone spoke.
fact: To check a meaning, swap in a simpler word like said and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «She uttered a cry when the door slammed.» What did she do? | *Made a sound with her voice | Ran out of the room | Closed the door quietly | Wrote a letter
exercise: «The old man uttered a single word and then closed his eyes.» How many words did he say? | *One | Three | None at all | Lots of words
exercise: «He never uttered a word all through the long meeting.» What does this mean? | He sang a song | *He stayed silent | He shouted loudly | He gave a long speech
test: Mia uttered a soft "hello" at the door. What did Mia do? | *Said hello quietly | Shouted at the door | Knocked three times | Left without a sound
test: Which word is the past tense of utter? | Utterly | Utter-ing | *Uttered | Utters
exam: Why might a writer choose uttered instead of said? | It makes the story longer and more exciting | It means the person was asleep | *It shows a short sound or only a few words were spoken | It means the person was running fast
